      "text": "We start by identifying the given lengths of the legs of the right triangle: \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\nFirst, we express \\(\\log_4 27\\) in terms of base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\log_{2^2} 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 2^2} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2} = \\frac{\\log_2 (3^3)}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nSo, \\(\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\).\n\nNext, we express \\(\\log_2 9\\) in terms of base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\nSo, \\(\\log_2 9 = 2 \\log_2 3\\).\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that the square of the hypotenuse \\(h\\) is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nh^2 = \\left( \\log_4 27 \\right)^2 + \\left( \\log_2 9 \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2.\n\\]\nCombining the terms over a common denominator, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2 + 16 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} = \\frac{25 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} = \\left( \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2.\n\\]\nThus, the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\) is:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nWe need to compute \\(4^h\\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5 = 243.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}.\n\\]",
