      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\) of the right triangle, we first need to determine the values of the legs given in the problem: \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\nFirst, we convert \\(\\log_4 27\\) to a base 2 logarithm. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\nSo, the length of one leg of the triangle is \\(\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\).\n\nNext, we convert \\(\\log_2 9\\) to a base 4 logarithm. Using the change of base formula again, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = \\frac{\\log_4 9}{\\log_4 2} = \\frac{\\log_4 3^2}{1} = 2 \\log_4 3\n\\]\nSo, the length of the other leg of the triangle is \\(2 \\log_4 3\\).\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\):\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\log_4 27 \\right)^2 + \\left( \\log_2 9 \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 2 \\log_4 3 \\right)^2}\n\\]\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( 2 \\log_4 3 \\right)^2 = 4 (\\log_4 3)^2 = 4 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 = 4 \\cdot \\frac{(\\log_2 3)^2}{4} = (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)}{4} + (\\log_2 3) \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{13 \\log_2 3}{4} \\right)^2} = \\frac{13 \\log_2 3}{4}\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(4^h\\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{13 \\log_2 3}{4}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{13 \\log_2 3}{4}} = 2^{\\frac{13 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{13}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{13}{2}} = 3^6 \\cdot 3^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 729 \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(4^h\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{729\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
